      "id": 1,
      "text": "Litchfield was named in 1734 after George Henry Lee, Earl of Lichfield, when the town separated from Dunstable.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www2.nhes.nh.gov/GraniteStats/SessionServlet?page=Community.jsp&SID=1&city=000615&cityName=Litchfield",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town officially joined New Hampshire on June 5, 1749, after having been part of Massachusetts for fifteen years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Litchfield%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before European settlers arrived, the Abenaki people inhabited the area that is now Litchfield.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Litchfield%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Archaeologists have uncovered more than 30 Native American sites along the Merrimack River in Litchfield, with artifacts dating back thousands of years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Litchfield%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The highest point in town is Rocky Hill, a 357‑foot summit in Litchfield State Forest near the town’s center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
